\section{Analysis 1: Motion Style Across Recordings}
\label{sec:a1}

\paragraph{Hypothesis.}
Motion statistics from one recording may help characterize the same person in
another. We expect same-person references to be closer to a query than
different-person references matched for speaking activity. This tests an
assumption behind reference conditioning and multi-reference training in
Ideas~1 and~2.

\paragraph{Method.}
We counted usable windows and distinct recordings per participant and retained
participants with separate query and reference recordings. SMPL-H body poses
were replayed on one fixed skeleton (the SMPL neutral mean-shape rest joints),
so body shape is identical for everyone, and positions were expressed in the
chest (spine3) frame. Each 5\,s block was described by left and right wrist
speed, left and right wrist excursion (RMS distance from the block mean), left
and right hand height, and head angular speed. Blocks needed at least 95\%
valid SMPL-H frames, and derivatives used only consecutive valid frames inside
a block. A 10\,s window is the equal-weight mean of its two blocks. Speeds and
excursions were log-transformed, and every descriptor was z-scored with
statistics fitted on the 16 single-recording participants, who never serve as
queries.

For each query window, the same-person reference is a window of the same person
in a different recording. Different-person references are all windows of other
participants whose speaking fraction (from the released VAD) lies within $0.10$
of the same-person reference. All windows last 10\,s, so durations are matched.
Distance is Euclidean in the 7-dimensional scaled space, and different-person
distances are averaged per participant before averaging across participants.
The per-query difference is $\Delta = \bar d_{\text{diff}} - d_{\text{same}}$,
where positive values favor the hypothesis. We also report a win rate, the
fraction of matched different-person windows farther from the query than the
same-person reference, which is $0.5$ at chance. Same-recording comparisons use
non-adjacent windows (at least 10\,s apart) of the query's own recording. We
averaged $\Delta$ per participant and computed 95\% intervals with 10{,}000
bootstrap resamples over participants.

For reference selection, we compared one continuous 10\,s clip with two
5\,s clips separated by at least 10\,s. Both clips come from one other recording
of the same person, so both conditions see one recording context. Different-person
references were built the same way and matched on speaking fraction, and we
used 40 random draws per query and condition.

\paragraph{Results.}
\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_primary.pdf}
  \caption{Analysis 1. (a) Valid 10\,s windows per participant, with shading
  alternating by recording; blue participants have at least two recordings.
  (b) Participant-level $\Delta$ for cross-recording and same-recording
  references, with bootstrap means and 95\% intervals; hollow points are the
  two participants who also have a recording in a second session. (c)
  Cross-recording $\Delta$ computed from one descriptor at a time. (d) One
  continuous 10\,s reference versus two non-adjacent 5\,s references from the
  same source recording; lines connect participants.}
  \label{fig:a1}
\end{figure*}

The three archives contain 38 participant-side recordings from 25 participants
(110.5 minutes). Of 1{,}307 five-second blocks, 1{,}257 (96\%) passed the
validity threshold, giving 610 valid windows. Only nine participants have two
or more recordings, and for seven of them every recording comes from the same
session (Fig.~\ref{fig:a1}a).

Across recordings, the same-person advantage is small and uncertain:
$\Delta = 0.15$ $[-0.13, 0.42]$, with six of nine participants positive and a
win rate of $0.53$ $[0.46, 0.60]$. Within a recording the advantage is clear:
$\Delta = 0.72$ $[0.34, 1.19]$, with eight of nine participants positive and a
win rate of $0.66$ $[0.58, 0.75]$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:a1}b). Restricted to pairs
from the same session, the cross-recording estimate is $0.21$
$[-0.11, 0.46]$ for eight participants. The two participants with a recording
in another session both have negative cross-recording $\Delta$ (mean $-0.27$).
No single descriptor has an interval excluding zero; head angular speed gives
the largest estimate, $0.09$ $[-0.04, 0.20]$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:a1}c).

Reference selection made no measurable difference. The continuous condition
gives $\Delta = 0.19$ $[-0.13, 0.48]$ and the spread condition $0.18$
$[-0.14, 0.49]$, a paired difference of $-0.01$ $[-0.07, 0.06]$ over nine
participants (Fig.~\ref{fig:a1}d).

\emph{Failure example.} Participant P0074 has the most negative cross-recording
$\Delta$ ($-0.63$) and only nine valid windows in each of two recordings from
one session. Participants P0047 and P0048 are each other's partners in one
interaction and each appear in a second session; both are closer to other
people than to themselves across recordings. With so few windows per recording,
recording-specific factors such as seating, table height, and conversation
topic can outweigh any stable individual habit in these coarse statistics.

\paragraph{Discussion.}
The descriptors separate people reliably only within a recording. That pattern
is what we expected if utterance or recording context, rather than a stable
movement habit, drives much of the similarity. The cross-recording estimate is
positive but its interval includes zero, and the only evidence across sessions
(two participants) points the other way. These archives therefore do not yet
show that a reference from a different recording carries person-specific
motion information that window-level kinematic statistics can detect. The
analysis also has little power. Nine participants, mostly confined to one
session, are too few to separate a weak effect from none, so we are extending
it to more archives and to participants with multiple sessions.

For Idea~1, a reference drawn from the query's own recording would make
personalization look stronger than it is, because it shares recording context
with the target. We will draw references only from other recordings and
preferably other sessions, and report same-recording results only as an upper
bound. For Idea~2, spreading the reference across two clips of one recording
did not help, so a benefit from multiple references should come from varied
recording contexts rather than from more time points in the same recording.
Multi-reference training therefore requires participants with several
sessions, and the data selection should target them explicitly instead of
following archive order. Finally, if coarse statistics remain at chance with
more participants, the reference encoder may need finer-grained features (hand
shape, gesture timing relative to speech) rather than the summary kinematics
tested here.
